DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       HHllffssdd  is  a daemon which implements a filesystem containing a symbolic
       link to subdirectory within a user’s home directory, depending  on  the
       user  which  accessed that link.  It was primarily designed to redirect
       incoming mail to users’ home directories, so that it can read from any‐
       where.

       HHllffssdd operates by mounting itself as an NFS server  for  the  directory
       containing _l_i_n_k_n_a_m_e, which defaults to //hhllffss//hhoommee.  Lookups within that
       directory  are  handled by hhllffssdd, which uses the password map to deter‐
       mine how to resolve the lookup.  The directory will be  created  if  it
       doesn’t  already  exist.   The  symbolic  link will be to the accessing
       user’s home directory, with _s_u_b_d_i_r appended to it.  If  not  specified,
       _s_u_b_d_i_r defaults to ..hhllffssddiirr.  This directory will also be created if it
       does not already exist.

       A SIGTERM sent to hhllffssdd will cause it to shutdown.  A SIGHUP will flush
       the  internal  caches, and reload the password map.  It will also close
       and reopen the log file, to enable the original log file to be  removed
       or rotated.  A SIGUSR1 will cause it to dump its internal table of user
       IDs and home directories to the file //uussrr//ttmmpp//hhllffssdd..dduummpp..XXXXXXXXXXXX.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --aa _a_l_t___d_i_r
              Alternate  directory.   The  name  of the directory to which the
              symbolic link returned by hhllffssdd will point, if it cannot  access
              the  home  directory  of  the user.  This defaults to //vvaarr//hhllffss.
              This directory will be created  if it doesn’t exist.  It is  ex‐
              pected  that  either  users will read these files, or the system
              administrators will run a script to resend this "lost  mail"  to
              its owner.

       --cc _c_a_c_h_e_‐_i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l
              Caching  interval.  HHllffssdd will cache the validity of home direc‐
              tories for this interval, in seconds.  Entries which  have  been
              verified within the last _c_a_c_h_e_‐_i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l seconds will not be ver‐
              ified again, since the operation could be expensive, and the en‐
              tries  are  most likely still valid.  After the interval has ex‐
              pired, hhllffssdd will re‐verify the validity of the user’s home  di‐
              rectory,  and  reset  the cache time‐counter.  The default value
              for _c_a_c_h_e_‐_i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l is 300 seconds (5 minutes).

       --ff     Force fast startup.  This option tells hhllffssdd  to  skip  startup‐
              time  consistency  checks  such as existence of mount directory,
              alternate spool directory, symlink to be hidden under the  mount
              directory, their permissions and validity.

       --gg _g_r_o_u_p
              Set  the special group HLFS_GID to _g_r_o_u_p.  Programs such as ffrroomm
              or ccoommssaatt, which access the mailboxes of other  users)  must  be
              setgid  HLFS_GID to work properly.  The default group is "hlfs".
              If no group is provided, and there is no group "hlfs", this fea‐
              ture is disabled.

       --hh     Help.  Print a brief help message, and exit.

       --ii _r_e_l_o_a_d_‐_i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l
              Map‐reloading interval.   Each  _r_e_l_o_a_d_‐_i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l  seconds,  hhllffssdd
              will  reload the password map.  HHllffssdd needs the password map for
              the UIDs  and  home  directory  pathnames.   HHllffssdd  schedules  a
              SIGALRM  to  reload  the  password maps.  A SIGHUP sent to hhllffssdd
              will force it to reload  the  maps  immediately.    The  default
              value for _r_e_l_o_a_d_‐_i_n_t_e_r_v_a_l is 900 seconds (15 minutes.)

       --ll _l_o_g_f_i_l_e
              Specify  a  log file to which hhllffssdd will record events.  If _l_o_g_‐
              _f_i_l_e is the string ssyysslloogg then the log messages will be sent  to
              the  system log daemon by _s_y_s_l_o_g(3), using the LOG_DAEMON facil‐
              ity.  This is also the default.

       --nn     No verify.  HHllffssdd will not verify the validity of  the  symbolic
              link  it  will  be  returning, or that the user’s home directory
              contains sufficient disk‐space for spooling.  This can speed  up
              hhllffssdd  at  the cost of possibly returning symbolic links to home
              directories which are not currently accessible or are full.   By
              default,  hhllffssdd  validates  the symbolic‐link in the background.
              The --nn option overrides the meaning of the --cc option,  since  no
              caching is necessary.

       --oo _m_o_u_n_t_‐_o_p_t_i_o_n_s
              Mount  options.  Mount options which hhllffssdd will use to mount it‐
              self on top of _d_i_r_n_a_m_e_.  By default,  _m_o_u_n_t_‐_o_p_t_i_o_n_s  is  set  to
              "ro".  If the system supports symbolic‐link caching, default op‐
              tions are set to "ro,nocache".

       --pp     Print  PID.   Outputs the process‐id of hhllffssdd to standard output
              where it can be saved into a file.

       --vv     Version.  Displays version information to standard error.

       --xx _l_o_g_‐_o_p_t_i_o_n_s
              Specify run‐time logging options.  The options are a comma sepa‐
              rated list chosen from: fatal, error,  user,  warn,  info,  map,
              stats, all.

       --CC     Force  hhllffssdd  to run on systems that cannot turn off the NFS at‐
              tribute‐cache.  Use of this option on those systems is  discour‐
              aged, as it may result in loss or mis‐delivery of mail.  The op‐
              tion  is  ignored  on  systems  that can turn off the attribute‐
              cache.

       --DD _l_o_g_‐_o_p_t_i_o_n_s
              Select from a variety of debugging options.  Prefixing an option
              with the string nnoo reverses the effect of that option.   Options
              are  cumulative.  The most useful option is aallll.  Since this op‐
              tion is only used for debugging other options are not documented
              here.  A fuller description is available in the program  source.
              A SIGUSR1 sent to hhllffssdd will cause it to dump its internal pass‐
              word map to the file //uussrr//ttmmpp//hhllffssdd..dduummpp..XXXXXXXXXXXX.

       --PP _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d_‐_f_i_l_e
              Read the user‐name, user‐id, and home directory information from
              the file _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d_‐_f_i_l_e_.  Normally, hhllffssdd will use _g_e_t_p_w_e_n_t(3) to
              read  the password database.  This option allows you to override
              the default database, and is useful if you want  to  map  users’
              mail files to a directory other than their home directory.  Only
              the  username,  uid, and home‐directory fields of the file _p_a_s_s_‐
              _w_o_r_d_‐_f_i_l_e are read and checked.  All other fields  are  ignored.
              The  file  _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d_‐_f_i_l_e  must  otherwise be compliant with Unix
              System 7 colon‐delimited format _p_a_s_s_w_d(5).

